---
// Custom Raylu case study. Notion drives metadata; this file owns the
// body. Spine: pre-PMF prototyping has a different success metric than
// shipped product. The deliverable is alignment, not polish. The
// Figma file IS the deck. That positioning is what closed $4M seed.

import CSLayout from "../../components/case-study/CSLayout.astro";
import CSHero from "../../components/case-study/CSHero.astro";
import CSSection from "../../components/case-study/CSSection.astro";
import CSPullquote from "../../components/case-study/CSPullquote.astro";
import CSStatGrid from "../../components/case-study/CSStatGrid.astro";
import CSLiveDemo from "../../components/case-study/CSLiveDemo.astro";
import CSFigure from "../../components/case-study/CSFigure.astro";
import RayluFigmaTimeline from "../../components/case-study/showcase/RayluFigmaTimeline.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<CSLayout
  title="Raylu — Jon Tollefson"
  description="Sketches to seed funding. Six-month design engagement with Raylu — brand, Figma component system, clickable prototypes that closed $4M seed."
  ogImage="/case-studies/raylu/cover.png"
>
  <CSHero
    eyebrow="Case study 04 · April 2023 — October 2023"
    title="Raylu"
    subtitle="Sketches to seed funding."
    cover="/case-studies/raylu/cover.png"
    meta={[
      { label: "Role", value: "Sole designer, working with the founders" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "Apr — Oct 2023 · ~6 months" },
      { label: "Stack", value: "Figma · React handoff" },
      { label: "Outcome", value: "$4M seed · Conversion Capital, Unusual Ventures, angels" },
    ]}
  />

  {/* ─────── 01 · THE PITCH ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="01" title="The pitch" deck="Raylu was an early-stage AI tooling company building UI for ML pipeline observability. The founders had product depth and technical clarity. They didn't have visual design. A Harvard contact made the intro." noTopRule>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>What started as a wireframe pass for a pitch deck extended into a six-month engagement: brand identity, a Figma component system, and clickable prototypes for fundraising and early-adopter conversations. The work covered everything between a founder sketch and a React-ready component library.</p>

      <p>The interesting bet of the engagement was the success metric. Pre-PMF prototyping is not shipped product. The brief was "evidence the product is real enough to fund and build." Polish costs founder burn rate; clarity of concept doesn't. That trade-off shaped every decision.</p>

      <CSPullquote hero>
        The Figma file is the deck. The deliverable is alignment, not polish.
      </CSPullquote>
    </div>

    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 4, label: "M seed raised", prefix: "$", color: "green" },
        { n: 6, label: "month engagement", color: "white" },
        { n: 2, label: "investors led the round", color: "cyan" },
        { n: 8, label: "M follow-on Series A in 2025", prefix: "$", color: "yellow" },
      ]}
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 02 · THE SEAM ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="02" title="What the founders had — and didn't" deck="Two cofounders, technical and product-fluent. Sketches that captured the system clearly. No brand, no component vocabulary, no clickable prototype that would survive an investor demo.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The gap was alignment. Sketches and Zoom calls produce ideas. They don't produce shared context across founders, potential investors, and an external designer. Without a designed artifact, every investor conversation re-derived the product from a whiteboard.</p>

      <p>The first deliverable wasn't a screen. It was the Figma file as alignment artifact — a workspace stakeholders could navigate without designer intervention, with linear sections for brand, competitor analysis, strategy, and the product itself. Founders could self-serve pitch updates. I stopped being the bottleneck for every external conversation.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 03 · THE WORKSPACE ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="03" title="The Figma workspace as alignment artifact" deck="A linear timeline that anyone could open and read top to bottom. Brand. Competitive analysis. Strategy. Wireframes. Hi-fi. Use the chevrons or click a section.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · SCROLL THE TIMELINE"
      caption="The real Figma file ran the same shape — vertically expanding sections, each one named, dated, and self-contained. Investors could open it cold and exit with the right mental model. The designer stopped being the bottleneck for context."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <RayluFigmaTimeline />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The compounding value was that the workspace doubled as the source of truth for the engineering team. Component patterns (nav, modals, form structure) mapped 1:1 to the React implementation. When the engineering team started, they pulled directly from the same file the founders had been pitching with — no separate handoff document, no design system migration. The artifact carried forward.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 04 · SKETCHES TO MOCKUPS ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="04" title="Sketches to mockups, in loops" deck="Founder sketches translated through Zoom-based exploration sessions into concrete goals for look, feel, and functionality. Iterative low-fi to hi-fi loops kept founders close to decisions without becoming a bottleneck.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/raylu/notion-35fed8d4704f808d9ca0f53a5b38a89d.png"
      alt="A founder sketch for the model-prototype starting page. A sidebar with three abstract icons, a chat-style interface at the top with a placeholder message and 'submit prompt below' note, and a grid labeled 'All systems' with eight 'Recommendation System' cards. Annotated in red: 'either talk to chat bot - or boot up premade system.'"
      caption="The sketch above set the V0 nav model. Two starting paths — talk to a chat bot, or boot a premade system. Hi-fi pulled this verbatim into a working Figma frame the same afternoon. The wireframe-to-mockup loop kept founder intent visible at every step."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />

    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/raylu/notion-35fed8d4704f80d780aaf0ab65e049a7.png"
      alt="Strategy slide. Header: raylu. Headline: Goal — provide necessary tools to make ML safe, with the word safe circled. Below: Machine Learning splits into Deterministic (defined scopes of inputs and outputs, simple linear regression) and Unbounded (no limitations of inputs, large language models, recommenders — targeting this second type). Right rail: observability — how the model was used, what it did, how it was received. We give companies the ability to wrap their in-production models in order to receive detailed context data linked with model performance. Instead of using a model as a stand-alone solution, sophisticated use-cases require chaining of models, intermediary processing, and human inserted rules to override models. For this reason, we think of machine learning as pipelines of connected nodes rather than a single model."
      caption="The strategy slide that anchored every screen downstream. Pipelines, not single models. Human-inserted rules over the chain. Once that thesis was in the file, every later mockup had something to be loyal to."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 05 · THE PIPELINE UI ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="05" title="The pipeline UI" deck="The core product surface was a workflow builder for AI/ML model pipelines. Initial nav split the app across Pipelines, Deployments, and Analytics. Detail screens covered pipeline creation, data connection, environment config, and scheduling.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/raylu/notion-35fed8d4704f8005968fd121bdbfc1d8.png"
      alt="Raylu hi-fi UMAP slicing UI. Left rail: 'raylu' wordmark and three nav icons (home, dots, square). Main area: '500 data row(s)' count and a search-query field at the top, plus Display and Sort By buttons. Below: a sidebar with controls for Point size (1.0x), Opacity (1), Thumbnail size (1.8x), a Reduction algorithm selector set to UMAP, and an Annotation selector set to 'vehicle.' To the right: a 2D scatterplot with a green cluster boxed in dashed lines on the upper-left, a smaller green cluster mid-lower, and a large orange cluster on the right. A 'Model Integration' button sits in the top-right corner."
      caption="UMAP slicing was the moment investors stopped asking 'is this for real engineers?' Real ML primitives — point clouds, annotation overlays, reduction algorithms — in a UI a product manager could read."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />

    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/raylu/notion-35fed8d4704f802581d3cfb7be04a5c8.png"
      alt="Raylu label-errors workflow. Filters at the top: All data / Slices / Splits, then Slice name search, and a Label errors section with Roads, Non-RGB images, Water, Fields options. Active filters: false positive count between Min 0 and Max 28, Metadata is 'data_split', plus a confidence threshold (0.5) and IOU threshold (0.5). Below: a grid of 16 thumbnails with bounding boxes overlaid, each checkbox-selected, showing detected objects. '14 selected' button top right."
      caption="The label-errors workflow that ML teams actually need. Each thumbnail is a candidate the model got wrong; the bulk-select and threshold sliders make it act like an inbox rather than a research notebook."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 06 · WHAT IT BOUGHT ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="06" title="What it bought" deck="The deliverable held up where it mattered: investor demos, early-adopter conversations, engineering handoff.">
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Raylu closed a <strong>$4M seed round</strong> in late 2023, led by Conversion Capital and Unusual Ventures, with angels including Arash Ferdowsi (Dropbox), Diego Oppenheimer (DataRobot), and Trent Hedge (Pylon). The product has since pivoted (now an AI sourcing platform for private-market investors) and raised an <strong>$8M Series A in late 2025</strong>.</p>

      <p>Engineering handoff inherited the same Figma workspace the founders had been pitching from. Component patterns (nav, modals, form structure) mapped 1:1 to the React implementation. The patterns left behind for the React team were the parts that needed to scale; everything else was sketch-rate by design.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 07 · WHAT GENERALIZES ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="07" title="What generalizes" deck="Two moves I'd repeat on any early-stage engagement.">
    <ul class="generalize">
      <li>
        <strong>The workspace is the highest-leverage deliverable.</strong>
        Sketches and Zoom calls produce ideas, not shared context. A navigable workspace lets founders self-serve pitch updates and keeps the designer from being the bottleneck for every external conversation. The artifact is the alignment.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pre-PMF prototyping has a different success metric than shipped product.</strong>
        The brief is "evidence the product is real enough to fund and build," not "polished system." Scoping down polish at this stage protects founder burn rate and lets the product hypothesis lead. The parts that need to scale (component patterns the engineering team inherits) get the polish budget; everything else is sketch-rate by design.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p class="muted">Raylu pivoted significantly post-2023; the surfaces and brand documented here reflect the original 2023 vision, not the current product.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  <div class="cs-foot">
    <a href="/#work" class="cs-foot-back">← back to selected work</a>
  </div>
</CSLayout>

<style>
  .prose-block { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block > .cs-pullquote { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block .muted { color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.48); font-size: 14.5px; }

  .generalize {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 18px 0 28px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .generalize li {
    padding: 16px 18px;
    border: 1px solid var(--cs-line);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.02);
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.85);
  }
  .generalize li strong {
    display: block;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    margin-bottom: 6px;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
  }

  .cs-foot {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 64px 0 0;
  }
  .cs-foot-back {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.32);
    border-radius: 2px;
    transition: color 160ms ease, border-color 160ms ease, background 160ms ease;
  }
  .cs-foot-back:hover {
    color: #f5f1e8;
    border-color: var(--brand-orange);
    background: rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.08);
  }
</style>
